1
00:00:08,140 --> 00:00:10,138
Rym: It's Wednesday, January 3rd, 2024.

2
00:00:10,138 --> 00:00:10,299
Rym: I'm Rem.

3
00:00:14,575 --> 00:00:15,007
Scott: I'm Scott.

4
00:00:15,550 --> 00:00:16,773
Rym: And this is GeekNights.

5
00:00:16,814 --> 00:00:22,650
Rym: Tonight we're gonna review a bunch of stuff that only one of us has seen or read.

6
00:00:23,670 --> 00:00:24,668
Scott: Let's do this.

7
00:00:26,191 --> 00:00:31,030
Rym: So the other night, New Year's Day actually, Emily and I, we stayed home.

8
00:00:31,230 --> 00:00:33,338
Rym: We didn't do any New Year's thing.

9
00:00:33,378 --> 00:00:35,085
Rym: We stayed home, relaxed.

10
00:00:35,105 --> 00:00:36,330
Rym: It was a pretty good time.

11
00:00:36,651 --> 00:00:41,990
Rym: I feel like next year is the year for a big, crazy party, but this year felt like a really calm time.

12
00:00:43,151 --> 00:00:45,237
Rym: So that day, we just decided to go out for a walk.

13
00:00:45,357 --> 00:00:49,410
Rym: We had to pick up a little bit of medicine for the rabbit at the vet right over the bridge anyway.

14
00:00:49,870 --> 00:00:58,630
Rym: So we biked over the bridge on some city bikes, and then we just wandered around because there was something notable in Manhattan that we wanted to look at right over there.

15
00:00:59,271 --> 00:01:11,150
Rym: So the Animal Medical Center, which is the vet for Moogie, our rabbit, which happens to be possibly the best, most capable exotic animal hospital in the world.

16
00:01:11,550 --> 00:01:12,770
Rym: Just happens to be right there.

17
00:01:12,910 --> 00:01:18,430
Scott: I don't know if it's in the world, but it's clearly, it's a top, it's not a usual kind of place, right?

18
00:01:18,450 --> 00:01:29,809
Scott: If you're not in a major city, and I think even other major cities usually don't have, it's a very large building, and on the side of it, it's like, you know, Animal Hospital, right?

19
00:01:29,930 --> 00:01:41,290
Rym: It's not just, it's a full hospital, not just for regular, typical animals, but also exotic animals, and it's a research hospital, it's a non-profit, and it is like top tier.

20
00:01:42,030 --> 00:01:45,209
Scott: Yeah, it can handle basically every kind of animal there is.

21
00:01:45,590 --> 00:01:49,357
Scott: Yeah, like I remember one time- It's not just, most places just have a vet.

22
00:01:49,417 --> 00:01:54,450
Scott: Maybe farm places have, you know, the horse vet and the cow vet, but this is an everything vet.

23
00:01:55,233 --> 00:02:00,390
Scott: I don't know who brings their horse to New York City to get it checked, but you could bring basically any kind of animal.

24
00:02:01,110 --> 00:02:08,190
Rym: I remember one time we went there with Theo Den, because he was a little sick, and in the room next to us was someone with their kestrel.

25
00:02:08,370 --> 00:02:11,627
Scott: I mean, you know, they're probably still doing mostly dog biz, right?

26
00:02:11,647 --> 00:02:12,310
Scott: That's the most common.

27
00:02:12,310 --> 00:02:16,970
Rym: Yeah, it's mostly dogs, and also all the NYPD police dogs go there, which makes sense.

28
00:02:17,411 --> 00:02:24,910
Rym: So anyway, we stopped by there, but it's right at the entrance to a park that has been closed for a long time.

29
00:02:25,370 --> 00:02:30,150
Rym: New York, there's two sides of Manhattan, the east side and the west side.

30
00:02:30,490 --> 00:02:32,430
Scott: There are four sides, it's an oval.

31
00:02:32,730 --> 00:02:34,429
Rym: No, there's a top and a bottom and two sides.

32
00:02:35,570 --> 00:02:45,010
Rym: But basically, yeah, yeah, what I'm getting at is on the west side, there's a mixed use bike path that goes all the way from the bottom to the top uninterrupted, and it's really nice.

33
00:02:45,691 --> 00:02:53,088
Scott: It is the most used bike path, I think, and most trafficked in the world, United States, something, you know, whatever.

34
00:02:53,148 --> 00:02:54,110
Scott: It's used a lot.

35
00:02:54,890 --> 00:02:55,796
Rym: It's impressive.

36
00:02:55,836 --> 00:03:00,370
Rym: Like, if you've never seen it, whatever bike path you think- It's not that impressive, it's just a strip of pavement.

37
00:03:00,610 --> 00:03:03,908
Rym: It is impressive that that exists, considering how America is.

38
00:03:05,891 --> 00:03:13,210
Scott: The Ocean Parkway one was the first one, and it was amazingly impressive when it was new.

39
00:03:14,173 --> 00:03:19,430
Scott: And now it's basically just a highway with some broken concrete and some benches on the side.

40
00:03:21,390 --> 00:03:26,570
Rym: So the east side has a much more troubled history of trying to have paths like that.

41
00:03:26,690 --> 00:03:30,810
Rym: There's old paths that are blocked off because they've been slowly collapsing into the river.

42
00:03:31,772 --> 00:03:35,510
Rym: There are old paths that are blocked off for random reasons.

43
00:03:35,891 --> 00:03:43,790
Rym: There are parts where there's just no place to put a path, including the United Nations, which is prickly about stuff being behind it for reasons you can imagine.

44
00:03:46,231 --> 00:03:57,630
Scott: The west side was a lot easier to put stuff on because you have a lot of stuff like all these places for cruise ships to dock and things like that.

45
00:03:57,930 --> 00:04:02,970
Scott: Whereas the east, if you go up and down the east, you've got all these different interests and things.

46
00:04:03,331 --> 00:04:08,910
Scott: There's a park here, there's a housing project here, there's all kinds of shit in the way, all the way up.

47
00:04:09,350 --> 00:04:12,510
Scott: And each section has different hassles you gotta deal with.

48
00:04:14,671 --> 00:04:21,308
Rym: So if you go over the bridge into Manhattan on the east side, you can go north, but that's been blocked off for construction for years.

49
00:04:22,570 --> 00:04:27,490
Rym: Now I've been running through it anyway this whole time, and I was the Strava local legend until recently.

50
00:04:27,610 --> 00:04:29,929
Rym: All you have to do is hop six fences along the way, and it's really nice.

51
00:04:30,271 --> 00:04:33,750
Rym: Because it's all finished back there mostly, but it was just blocked off for reasons.

52
00:04:34,090 --> 00:04:37,910
Scott: They don't unblock it until it's truly done, done, done, done.

53
00:04:38,311 --> 00:04:40,030
Scott: And then they have a ribbon cutting or whatever.

54
00:04:40,110 --> 00:04:43,410
Scott: Then they unblock things, even if they're basically done.

55
00:04:44,574 --> 00:04:45,669
Rym: Like when we used to bike up.

56
00:04:45,770 --> 00:04:48,470
Scott: As long as people aren't doing construction while you're going through.

57
00:04:48,670 --> 00:04:59,830
Rym: Yeah, I remember every weekend if we biked through Van Cortlandt Park, someone cut the fence, or cut the gate, or cut the lock to open that little closed bit of the bike path.

58
00:05:00,010 --> 00:05:02,669
Rym: And then someone would seal it up, and then someone would open it again.

59
00:05:04,111 --> 00:05:07,830
Rym: But if you went south there, there really was nowhere to go south.

60
00:05:08,150 --> 00:05:15,610
Rym: Like there was an old promenade that is like busted up to a degree that it looks like it would fall apart if you so much as sneezed on it.

61
00:05:15,851 --> 00:05:18,790
Scott: No, the path south starts at like 30-something street.

62
00:05:18,870 --> 00:05:27,070
Scott: So basically from 50-something to 30-something there's like a 20 block gap where you have to use basically First Avenue to go south reasonably.

63
00:05:28,513 --> 00:05:33,730
Scott: You could go in Sutton Place a little bit south for like five blocks, and then you gotta go on First Avenue anyway.

64
00:05:34,671 --> 00:05:38,685
Rym: And there are, mixed in that area, there are some old paths.

65
00:05:40,610 --> 00:05:41,390
Scott: First Avenue goes north.

66
00:05:41,390 --> 00:05:45,650
Rym: But they're blocked off on either end because of the way the FDR was built and overbuilt over the years.

67
00:05:45,810 --> 00:05:50,130
Rym: So they're just like these blocked off inaccessible segments of what used to be pedestrian way.

68
00:05:50,210 --> 00:06:02,430
Rym: But anyway, they finally finish this super long project to build a new promenade from this park, which is right next to the medical center, all the way down to Sutton Place, that park.

69
00:06:03,192 --> 00:06:05,889
Rym: It's like out in the river, it's super wide.

70
00:06:06,171 --> 00:06:12,390
Scott: They close this gap of, there's that 20-something block gap, and now it's more like a 15 block gap.

71
00:06:13,730 --> 00:06:28,410
Rym: And while the city has said forever they intend to complete the path on the entire east side, for any of you who's lived in New York, there is a big step between the city saying it intends to do something and that thing happening.

72
00:06:28,450 --> 00:06:39,170
Scott: Yeah, if you look at all of the different city, state, even federal agencies, there are all sorts of plans, projects, things that people have written up.

73
00:06:40,210 --> 00:06:51,030
Scott: And it's like, if somehow all those things were done, the United States would be transformed into some miraculous paradise of some kind, with all this public infrastructure and facilities.

74
00:06:51,571 --> 00:06:56,197
Scott: But basically, people see those plans and they're like, "Ooh, this is going to

75
00:06:56,217 --> 00:06:56,578
Scott: happen.".

76
00:06:56,618 --> 00:07:04,870
Scott: But really, the idea is that the government employees make those plans for their agency, and that's what they get paid to do.

77
00:07:05,430 --> 00:07:09,517
Scott: And then what actually gets made is what gets money.

78
00:07:09,777 --> 00:07:17,050
Scott: And so it's up to the politicians of the day to decide which of the, you know, they look around and they're like, "What are we going to spend the money

79
00:07:17,110 --> 00:07:17,150
Scott: on?".

80
00:07:17,250 --> 00:07:19,412
Scott: Well, there's obviously all the things that already exist.

81
00:07:20,053 --> 00:07:23,516
Scott: And then it's like, "Okay, well, here's the money for things that are new.

82
00:07:24,237 --> 00:07:26,139
Scott: All these people have a million plans.

83
00:07:26,299 --> 00:07:27,120
Scott: Let's pick some of

84
00:07:27,180 --> 00:07:27,340
Scott: them.".

85
00:07:27,930 --> 00:07:28,131
Scott: Right?

86
00:07:28,371 --> 00:07:30,175
Scott: Will they actually get some money?

87
00:07:30,415 --> 00:07:33,641
Scott: Will they get enough money to proceed and actually happen?

88
00:07:34,082 --> 00:07:38,130
Scott: Will they get enough money to, like, enhance the plan but not actually do the plan?

89
00:07:38,751 --> 00:07:45,929
Scott: You know, it's like if you're the employee at an agency and your plan got some money, it's like, "Ooh, I'm going to get a promotion.

90
00:07:45,949 --> 00:07:46,069
Scott: All

91
00:07:46,089 --> 00:07:46,290
Scott: right.".

92
00:07:46,870 --> 00:07:48,073
Rym: And there's also two kinds of plans.

93
00:07:48,093 --> 00:07:54,230
Rym: There's the kind of plan that is, this is what will happen as stages are funded, but stages might just never get funded.

94
00:07:54,611 --> 00:07:55,994
Rym: And then there's the plan Scott described.

95
00:07:56,014 --> 00:08:03,470
Rym: So anyway, while the city has said forever they're going to make a path that goes all the way up and down the east side, it's coming in dribs and drabs.

96
00:08:03,991 --> 00:08:10,546
Rym: But what was very interesting, this path is super overbuilt, the new one, the East Midtown Greenway.

97
00:08:10,907 --> 00:08:12,270
Rym: Like, it's really big.

98
00:08:13,911 --> 00:08:15,610
Scott: It's wide, but it's not very long.

99
00:08:16,271 --> 00:08:16,331
Rym: Yep.

100
00:08:16,351 --> 00:08:22,710
Rym: It dead ends suddenly and does a sharp right turn at, like, 51st Street, and then it's a bunch of nonsense.

101
00:08:24,270 --> 00:08:29,949
Rym: Directly under it is one of those abandoned paths that you can get to, and there is a bridge that goes to it.

102
00:08:30,890 --> 00:08:38,789
Rym: But there's a big flat spot that is very obviously intended for this to continue if they ever build the next segment.

103
00:08:39,270 --> 00:08:40,214
Rym: It's, like, set up.

104
00:08:40,335 --> 00:08:43,830
Rym: You can see where the next segment would connect to the existing segment.

105
00:08:44,311 --> 00:08:51,630
Rym: And from that spot, you can see another thing that is right on the other side of the U.N.

106
00:08:52,231 --> 00:09:04,230
Rym: It's a thing called the East River Esplanade, which they built a while ago, which is extra weird because when they built it, you could basically cross a little bridge and be in this little esplanade by the river that is super long.

107
00:09:04,671 --> 00:09:10,730
Rym: Like, it goes, like, seven blocks and then dead ends, and there's only one way in and out all the way at one end, and it's just gigantic.

108
00:09:11,730 --> 00:09:31,189
Rym: And it ends at, like, 41st Street, and it is clear that the intention is to build another thing that connects the end of that to the end of this because that would bypass the U.N But right now, there is no plan to build that, so there's two paths that end in dead ends, and you can see them from each other behind the U.N

109
00:09:32,214 --> 00:09:34,550
Scott: Yeah, you know, when we're old people, this might happen.

110
00:09:34,910 --> 00:09:44,009
Rym: Yeah, but while we're walking over there, like, we see what can only be described as a field of light in the distance, and we're like, "What the fuck is

111
00:09:44,029 --> 00:09:44,310
Rym: that?".

112
00:09:44,711 --> 00:09:52,210
Rym: So we walk over, and over on that side of Manhattan, there's this giant empty lot that's been empty for as long as I've lived here.

113
00:09:52,471 --> 00:09:55,009
Rym: It's like this big open field that's got fences around it.

114
00:09:55,991 --> 00:10:03,850
Rym: And the field was absolutely filled with LEDs in a gigantic art installation called the Field of Light that I didn't even know was there.

115
00:10:04,590 --> 00:10:05,793
Rym: And we're like, "Ooh, what's

116
00:10:05,833 --> 00:10:06,154
Rym: this?".

117
00:10:06,274 --> 00:10:13,730
Rym: And we walk over, and there's a tiny line of people to get in, and there's a ton of paid employees standing around.

118
00:10:13,830 --> 00:10:15,178
Rym: They're like, "Oh, are you here for the Field of

119
00:10:15,198 --> 00:10:15,419
Rym: Light?".

120
00:10:15,439 --> 00:10:15,862
Rym: And we're like,

121
00:10:15,882 --> 00:10:16,868
Rym: "Yes.".

122
00:10:17,290 --> 00:10:18,175
Rym: And they're like, "Do you have a

123
00:10:18,215 --> 00:10:18,477
Rym: ticket?".

124
00:10:18,537 --> 00:10:19,060
Rym: And we're like,

125
00:10:20,186 --> 00:10:20,528
Rym: "No.".

126
00:10:21,271 --> 00:10:26,188
Rym: And they're like, "Oh, well, they're sold out, so you couldn't get one anyway, but you can stand here and wait and stand

127
00:10:26,228 --> 00:10:26,409
Rym: by.".

128
00:10:26,830 --> 00:10:30,103
Rym: And then he leans in, and he's like, "Everyone's getting in.

129
00:10:30,123 --> 00:10:30,906
Rym: It's gonna take, like, a

130
00:10:31,208 --> 00:10:31,489
Rym: minute.".

131
00:10:31,930 --> 00:10:37,710
Rym: And then they let us in, and it's just this giant art installation from this famous guy, and there's a whole thing that I'll link to.

132
00:10:38,111 --> 00:10:43,810
Rym: And we spent the night walking around this kind of beautiful art installation in a field in Manhattan.

133
00:10:44,511 --> 00:10:46,238
Scott: I gotta get in the art installation business.

134
00:10:46,278 --> 00:10:48,990
Scott: I have so many art installation ideas, right?

135
00:10:49,712 --> 00:10:51,357
Scott: It's like, what did the guy really do?

136
00:10:51,839 --> 00:10:55,670
Scott: He sat there, and he put LEDs on little sticks, and just got a fuck ton of them.

137
00:10:55,710 --> 00:10:57,136
Rym: Well, actually, it's a little more complex than that.

138
00:10:57,217 --> 00:11:00,270
Rym: It's actually fiber optics that lead to... Anyway, it's a whole thing.

139
00:11:01,110 --> 00:11:06,563
Scott: Right, but it's not something that's... It's obviously, you need to get the money to do it.

140
00:11:06,823 --> 00:11:08,307
Scott: You need to get the time to do it.

141
00:11:10,390 --> 00:11:16,730
Rym: You gotta have the name to convince a city that it's a good idea to do, and convince people to give you money to do it.

142
00:11:16,730 --> 00:11:22,265
Scott: Like, if I did the exact same fucking thing as that guy, and I paid for it myself somehow, right?

143
00:11:22,345 --> 00:11:23,949
Scott: It's like, would they let me do that?

144
00:11:24,009 --> 00:11:24,150
Scott: No.

145
00:11:24,470 --> 00:11:25,826
Scott: Would anyone buy tickets to it?

146
00:11:25,907 --> 00:11:26,089
Scott: No.

147
00:11:26,830 --> 00:11:27,674
Rym: The tickets are free.

148
00:11:28,378 --> 00:11:30,870
Rym: The city paid for it, as far as I can tell, as well as a bunch of charities.

149
00:11:31,411 --> 00:11:36,709
Rym: And at the entrance to it, there was a whole thing that was like, "Here's how this guy got this

150
00:11:36,729 --> 00:11:36,930
Rym: done.".

151
00:11:36,950 --> 00:11:46,949
Rym: And basically, like, 20 years ago, he did a really, really, really, really tiny one on his shoestring budget, and just kept doing the same thing bigger and bigger and bigger over time.

152
00:11:47,550 --> 00:11:49,577
Scott: There's a lot of people who do that.

153
00:11:49,638 --> 00:11:53,010
Scott: They got their thing, and they just keep doing it, and it's a good move.

154
00:11:53,991 --> 00:11:54,211
Rym: Yep.

155
00:11:54,232 --> 00:12:01,730
Rym: So, yeah, all that was a long way of saying, the best reason to live in New York City is that, I mean, what did we just talk about?

156
00:12:01,930 --> 00:12:04,099
Rym: We got one of the best animal medical centers in the world.

157
00:12:04,119 --> 00:12:06,870
Rym: We got one of the best bike paths inside of a city in the world.

158
00:12:07,311 --> 00:12:10,729
Rym: And if you wander around randomly, you'll just bump into art installations like this.

159
00:12:12,151 --> 00:12:13,154
Rym: It's a cool place to live.

160
00:12:14,438 --> 00:12:18,009
Rym: There's actually huge news that we can talk about, too.

161
00:12:20,291 --> 00:12:26,870
Rym: Mickey Mouse, at least, the OG Mickey Mouse, something I never actually thought would happen while I was alive.

162
00:12:27,870 --> 00:12:28,649
Rym: He's in the public domain.

163
00:12:30,413 --> 00:12:35,007
Scott: They extended copyright by 15 years, right, when they extended it, and when we were in college.

164
00:12:36,592 --> 00:12:42,150
Rym: I remember being an activist fighting against every extension of copyright that happened while I was alive.

165
00:12:43,350 --> 00:12:43,531
Scott: Yep.

166
00:12:43,672 --> 00:12:49,610
Scott: And then, you know, the Supreme Court was like, because the Constitution says it has to be limited.

167
00:12:49,730 --> 00:12:52,219
Scott: It has the word limited in the Constitution, right?

168
00:12:52,239 --> 00:12:55,530
Scott: It cannot be an infinitely long copyright license, right?

169
00:12:55,970 --> 00:13:04,396
Scott: So if they tried to write a law that said copyright lasts forever, the Supreme Court would say, even this Supreme Court would say, "No, that law is unconstitutional.

170
00:13:04,897 --> 00:13:05,639
Scott: It says limited.

171
00:13:06,080 --> 00:13:09,929
Scott: Even an originalist can't interpret that to mean that unlimited is

172
00:13:09,989 --> 00:13:10,150
Scott: okay.".

173
00:13:10,250 --> 00:13:12,670
Rym: Unless with this Supreme Court, a Republican just gave them money.

174
00:13:13,450 --> 00:13:17,683
Scott: I'm just saying, even this Supreme Court would, right?

175
00:13:17,984 --> 00:13:19,950
Scott: And the last time, they extended copyright.

176
00:13:20,630 --> 00:13:24,490
Scott: And there was, we talked about it when it happened, because it was that long ago, and Geek Nice is that old.

177
00:13:25,251 --> 00:13:40,550
Scott: They went to the Supreme Court, and Larry Lessig went to argue, and he said, "Hey, they're getting around the Constitution by, instead of making a law that says unlimited, they just keep making a new law with a larger term, effectively making it

178
00:13:40,590 --> 00:13:40,950
Scott: unlimited.".

179
00:13:41,050 --> 00:13:47,918
Scott: And the Supreme Court said, their decision was, very simply, right, "This is okay.

180
00:13:48,098 --> 00:13:58,389
Scott: This limit is okay, but if we see you go down the slippery slope anymore, we're gonna hint that we're gonna then maybe change our

181
00:13:58,429 --> 00:13:58,750
Scott: mind.".

182
00:14:00,113 --> 00:14:00,313
Scott: Right?

183
00:14:00,333 --> 00:14:06,030
Scott: So they had already gone down the slope quite a ways with extensions, and making copyright last longer.

184
00:14:06,491 --> 00:14:18,030
Scott: But last time, they sort of said, "This, hinting this is the last time, well, if you try to make it 90 years instead of 75 or whatever it is, we're gonna probably not look too kindly on

185
00:14:18,070 --> 00:14:18,230
Scott: that.".

186
00:14:18,670 --> 00:14:25,062
Scott: And so, you know, this government can't pass any legislation anyway, so copyright has not been extended further.

187
00:14:25,343 --> 00:14:29,250
Scott: We haven't had a new major copyright law, what, since the DMCA, basically, right?

188
00:14:29,370 --> 00:14:39,270
Rym: Yeah, because it sucks that our Congress can't do basic things, but the one benefit is- You mean things that are not, you know, controversial between the two parties?

189
00:14:39,310 --> 00:14:42,870
Rym: Like getting rid of Daylight Saving Time, where almost everyone in America wants to get rid of it?

190
00:14:43,730 --> 00:14:47,782
Scott: Right, it's like, this is not a thing that, you know, right-wing and left-wing people disagree on.

191
00:14:47,843 --> 00:14:50,270
