Scott: Alright, so now we'll talk about some stuff.

386
00:30:24,141 --> 00:30:29,680
Rym: I'll go first with good old Scott Pilgrim Takes Off, which I'm sure Scott will watch at some point.

387
00:30:29,680 --> 00:30:30,459
Rym: Because one, it's not long.

388
00:30:31,161 --> 00:30:35,980
Scott: I am currently paying for Netflix, so I should watch it before I stop paying to get my money's worth.

389
00:30:36,361 --> 00:30:37,159
Rym: One, it's not long.

390
00:30:37,600 --> 00:30:40,480
Rym: Two, it's Studio Saru.

391
00:30:40,760 --> 00:30:45,420
Rym: This is a full-on top production value anime anime.

392
00:30:45,981 --> 00:30:47,369
Rym: This is for real.

393
00:30:47,651 --> 00:30:49,079
Rym: They put the right amount of care into it.

394
00:30:49,722 --> 00:30:52,240
Rym: And three, it is not only fantastic.

395
00:30:53,521 --> 00:30:58,980
Rym: I actually would like to watch it again before too long, but I want to reread the comic first to do a little compare and contrast.

396
00:31:00,822 --> 00:31:07,720
Rym: It is more like, and I'm not the only one who's expressed this sentiment, the Evangelion reboot movies than you're expecting.

397
00:31:09,421 --> 00:31:14,480
Rym: And as you watch it, you might think, well, it did some things like what the Eva reboots did.

398
00:31:14,960 --> 00:31:21,160
Rym: Then it starts to directly reference Evangelion and then directly reference the reboots of Evangelion and then do some stuff.

399
00:31:22,681 --> 00:31:27,794
Rym: There's definitely some inspiration from how the reboot of Evangelion went down.

400
00:31:28,115 --> 00:31:29,940
Rym: that went into Scott Pilgrim Takes Off.

401
00:31:30,922 --> 00:31:33,960
Rym: And it's worth not knowing the deal.

402
00:31:34,700 --> 00:31:48,820
Rym: I would say that it wouldn't nearly, just like the Eva reboots, would not have as much impact if you weren't already familiar with the first and second and possibly third impacts of Evangelion, the original anime.

403
00:31:49,522 --> 00:31:56,120
Rym: Scott Pilgrim Takes Off is perfectly good standalone, but you'll get a lot more out of it if you have read the Scott Pilgrim comic first.

404
00:31:56,903 --> 00:31:58,419
Scott: I did read it, but a long time ago.

405
00:32:00,641 --> 00:32:02,526
Rym: I remember the comic pretty well.

406
00:32:02,546 --> 00:32:07,900
Rym: The only thing I'm kind of hazy on is the Utena stuff at the very end of the Scott Pilgrim comic.

407
00:32:08,040 --> 00:32:19,540
Rym: I forget exactly how that went down because the live action movie, which is also good and important to the canon of this, especially considering it's all the same voice actors from that movie play their own characters in this anime.

408
00:32:21,381 --> 00:32:35,880
Rym: But all three together are as important if you care about Scott Pilgrim to view or read as, if you care about Evangelion, you really should watch the movies, watch the show, and read the manga.

409
00:32:36,020 --> 00:32:41,620
Rym: Because they're all different takes on the same core and they all complement each other extremely well.

410
00:32:41,800 --> 00:32:46,428
Scott: Well, I think Evangelion, most of its renditions in different mediums have value and hold up today.

411
00:32:54,480 --> 00:33:00,452
Scott: Whereas if Scott Pilgrim, if someone said, "Hey, should I read the Scott Pilgrim comic in the year

412
00:33:00,452 --> 00:33:00,492
Scott: 2024?"

413
00:33:00,492 --> 00:33:00,933
Scott: It's like, "Um,

414
00:33:00,953 --> 00:33:01,093
Scott: maybe.".

415
00:33:05,581 --> 00:33:08,951
Rym: If I reread the first one again, I would say yes.

416
00:33:09,934 --> 00:33:12,060
Rym: Partly because a lot of people...

417
00:33:12,100 --> 00:33:15,680
Scott: It's not some all-time strong thing like Evangelion TV.

418
00:33:16,080 --> 00:33:18,555
Rym: No, I mean, it's not... While it shares a lot...

419
00:33:19,521 --> 00:33:20,230
Scott: It has issues.

420
00:33:20,331 --> 00:33:21,080
Scott: It has a lot of issues.

421
00:33:21,180 --> 00:33:23,638
Rym: I mean, Evangelion TV also does have a lot of issues.

422
00:33:24,362 --> 00:33:26,060
Scott: Not like the way Scott Pilgrim has issues.

423
00:33:26,401 --> 00:33:35,420
Rym: Well, that's why it's actually worth reading, I think, because there's a lot that happens in the anime that I'm really going to get into for spoilery reasons.

424
00:33:35,780 --> 00:33:50,860
Rym: But it directly interrogates every specific criticism of the original Scott Pilgrim comic that you can think of in a very profound way that actually enhances what was happening in the comic.

425
00:33:51,180 --> 00:34:01,980
Rym: While also ensuring that anyone who read the comic or watches this, the way I'll put it, they will take the correct moral away and not the incorrect moral.

426
00:34:03,460 --> 00:34:12,120
Rym: Because the incorrect morals from the Scott Pilgrim comic were specifically not intended by Brian Lee O'Malley.

427
00:34:12,340 --> 00:34:13,788
Rym: He has said as much repeatedly.

428
00:34:13,808 --> 00:34:16,020
Rym: A lot of people who read the first half of Scott Pilgrim...

429
00:34:16,040 --> 00:34:18,820
Scott: He was just very young and not that as good at writing.

430
00:34:19,268 --> 00:34:21,810
Scott: But also... Think about how long ago that was.

431
00:34:21,891 --> 00:34:22,958
Scott: How much younger he was.

432
00:34:23,820 --> 00:34:24,782
Rym: He said this much, too.

433
00:34:24,842 --> 00:34:32,260
Rym: But also, that comic, a lot of people who have complaints about it seem to focus on the first two-thirds, which is fair.

434
00:34:32,860 --> 00:34:41,335
Rym: But they seem to never really bring up how the last third of the comic sort of points out, "Yeah, all of that was from Scott Pilgrim's perspective.

435
00:34:41,775 --> 00:34:43,158
Rym: Was he really the good guy?

436
00:34:43,197 --> 00:34:43,880
Rym: What's really going

437
00:34:43,920 --> 00:34:43,960
Rym: on?".

438
00:34:44,703 --> 00:34:47,600
Rym: It tried to do something, but I think a lot of people missed it.

439
00:34:48,040 --> 00:34:54,494
Rym: Scott Pilgrim takes off, basically zooms in and says, "Yeah, here's what you were supposed to take away from it.

440
00:34:54,655 --> 00:34:56,699
Rym: And just in case he didn't get it, watch

441
00:34:56,739 --> 00:34:56,940
Rym: this.".

442
00:34:57,220 --> 00:35:03,914
Rym: Just like the Eva movies, the reboots are very much like, "Yeah, the morals are the same even if the events are very different.

443
00:35:04,074 --> 00:35:06,178
Rym: Just in case you missed it, Gendo's not

444
00:35:06,218 --> 00:35:06,479
Rym: cool.".

445
00:35:07,762 --> 00:35:08,945
Scott: Yeah, it's like, "We're gonna do this

446
00:35:09,005 --> 00:35:09,366
Scott: again.".

447
00:35:09,687 --> 00:35:12,535
Scott: And it's like the Evangelion movies, the 1.11s and 3s or whatever.

448
00:35:15,220 --> 00:35:21,440
Scott: They start off with literally just reanimating the same thing until the last 30 seconds of the first movie.

449
00:35:22,280 --> 00:35:22,481
Scott: Right.

450
00:35:22,801 --> 00:35:25,248
Scott: And then they take a left turn.

451
00:35:25,328 --> 00:35:29,980
Scott: And so Scott Pilgrim, from what I've heard, is that the first episode does that same thing.

452
00:35:31,082 --> 00:35:34,507
Scott: It looks like the comic, and then... No, it's not.

453
00:35:34,548 --> 00:35:40,157
Scott: Because if they just animated that original comic page for page, that would not be good.

454
00:35:40,317 --> 00:35:43,640
Scott: We don't need that in 2024.

455
00:35:43,640 --> 00:35:54,120
Scott: And so I do like this... It used to be where the kind of person who would be, "Ah, you've adapted the thing, but you have not held true to the original

456
00:35:54,180 --> 00:35:54,280
Scott: thing.".

457
00:35:55,562 --> 00:36:05,300
Scott: But it's like all those people who committed that crime were actually just being sort of close to the original and changing important things.

458
00:36:05,861 --> 00:36:12,659
Scott: Like, "We're gonna tell the same basic Spider-Man story, but we're gonna just change some stuff but not too much

459
00:36:12,699 --> 00:36:12,940
Scott: stuff.".

460
00:36:15,161 --> 00:36:17,085
Scott: What's the point of changing just a few things?

461
00:36:17,887 --> 00:36:21,434
Scott: You're not saying anything by doing that.

462
00:36:21,474 --> 00:36:23,980
Scott: You're just changing some things for why.

463
00:36:25,420 --> 00:36:41,300
Scott: But here, if you're gonna redo, drastic redo, and you can actually comment on your own work or even correct the work of the past or enhance or elaborate on it or add in some way.

464
00:36:41,781 --> 00:36:48,417
Rym: And it does all of those things to a similar level of skill as how the EVA reboot movies do to EVA.

465
00:36:48,738 --> 00:36:49,620
Rym: But again, not saying...

466
00:36:50,080 --> 00:36:53,000
Scott: As opposed to, also, people would just make sequels.

467
00:36:53,800 --> 00:37:00,580
Rym: Because look now, we've got a live-action movie, the anime, and the comic, and they are all very different takes on the same core.

468
00:37:02,862 --> 00:37:14,481
Scott: It's like you think with Star Wars, it's like, would they maybe have been better off instead of making Star Wars episodes 1, 2, 3, 7, 8, 9?

469
00:37:14,481 --> 00:37:23,400
Scott: Or would they have been better off just making Star Wars that starts off in Tatooine, Moss Eyes League, right?

470
00:37:24,381 --> 00:37:28,936
Scott: And then, it's like, oh, left turn, this is 2024 Star Wars.

471
00:37:29,056 --> 00:37:30,220
Scott: It's the same, but it's not.

472
00:37:30,980 --> 00:37:31,722
Rym: Yup.

473
00:37:31,863 --> 00:37:36,635
Rym: Oh, they didn't get the Death Star, and Vixen Wedge is still around, and something else.

474
00:37:36,655 --> 00:37:38,319
Rym: Now Obi-Wan is gonna save the day.

475
00:37:38,359 --> 00:37:38,800
Rym: Luke's dead.

476
00:37:39,622 --> 00:37:48,760
Scott: Yeah, they could have done, but I think being live-action also, the people doing this, they're all animated comics and things.

477
00:37:50,001 --> 00:37:53,479
Scott: If it's live-action, people are dead, people are old.

478
00:37:54,300 --> 00:37:55,363
Rym: That's where this worked out.

479
00:37:55,404 --> 00:38:00,900
Rym: They couldn't easily make another live-action movie with the actors who played, because they're all old now.

480
00:38:01,060 --> 00:38:05,164
Rym: Like Michael Cera could not look like Scott Pilgrim in the year 2024.

481
00:38:05,164 --> 00:38:05,878
Scott: No, you can't do that.

482
00:38:06,361 --> 00:38:11,112
Rym: But, because it's animated, it is all the original voice actors.

483
00:38:11,132 --> 00:38:14,580
Rym: You got Michael Cera, you got Brie Larson, Chris Evans.

484
00:38:14,780 --> 00:38:19,358
Rym: All these A-listers came back to voice-act this anime because they were into it.

485
00:38:20,483 --> 00:38:22,277
Rym: And because it was animated, it doesn't matter what they look like.

486
00:38:24,183 --> 00:38:28,880
Rym: Beyond all that, even if we forget the whole Scott Pilgrim-iverse, it's Studio Saru.

487
00:38:30,200 --> 00:38:30,546
Scott: Science Saru.

488
00:38:31,501 --> 00:38:33,749
Rym: They go all out, every episode.

489
00:38:33,930 --> 00:38:36,500
Rym: The animation is, I would say, phenomenal.

490
00:38:37,704 --> 00:38:40,160
Scott: They don't think they've ever had a bad animation.

491
00:38:40,180 --> 00:38:45,760
Rym: Yeah, it is just a feast for the eyes, and the episodes all have different kinds of styles.

492
00:38:45,960 --> 00:38:51,468
Rym: Some of them go sort of trigger-esque, some of them are very zoomed in on human... It's all over the place.

493
00:38:52,253 --> 00:38:53,520
Rym: Everything about it is great.

494
00:38:53,920 --> 00:38:55,960
Scott: The Trigger, Delicious, and Dungeon is almost here.

495
00:38:56,262 --> 00:38:58,420
Rym: Oh, I'm actually really looking forward to that.

496
00:38:59,244 --> 00:39:00,048
Scott: Okay, so... Yeah.

497
00:39:02,800 --> 00:39:04,869
Rym: So yeah, that's my review of Scott Pilgrim Takes Off.

498
00:39:04,909 --> 00:39:07,440
Rym: We might do a deeper one when Scott watches it at some point.

499
00:39:08,001 --> 00:39:12,380
Scott: So, speaking of things that are... Those are remakes, but let's talk about some sequels.

500
00:39:13,120 --> 00:39:14,083
Scott: Sort of sequels.

501
00:39:14,103 --> 00:39:20,339
Scott: Because a common thing that happens in anime and manga is things have arcs.

502
00:39:22,480 --> 00:39:24,399
Scott: JoJo's Bizarre Adventure has the most arcs.

503
00:39:25,526 --> 00:39:27,960
Scott: But even something like Sailor Moon has got some arcs.

504
00:39:28,420 --> 00:39:32,759
Rym: Yep, even Sailor Moon R has the tree arc and then the crucified people arc.

505
00:39:33,920 --> 00:39:34,462
Scott: Right.

506
00:39:34,482 --> 00:39:45,798
Scott: And a lot of times what happens when there's multiple arcs is that even though there's the same characters, except the ones who've died or not come back yet, is that fans will be like, "That arc's good, that arc's

507
00:39:45,858 --> 00:39:46,240
Scott: bad.".

508
00:39:46,420 --> 00:39:49,720
Rym: "Don't watch the Island episodes" is a common refrain for old people.

509
00:39:49,861 --> 00:39:50,081
Scott: Some

510
00:39:50,202 --> 00:39:50,483
Rym: show

511
00:39:50,603 --> 00:40:14,300
Scott: or manga will be really popular because of its initial arc, and then once things have to change because they can only carry that on for so long, further arcs may just be more of the same, just keep it going, or they have to take a big sharp turn to mix up something new, and often when they take that sharp turn, it ends up being a disappointment, right?

512
00:40:14,340 --> 00:40:16,120
Scott: The notable one being Death Note, right?

513
00:40:17,285 --> 00:40:28,520
Scott: Death Note, it's like you have these main thing you're really into, and then like halfway through the manga, it's like they sort of resolve the initial plot, because how much longer could they possibly keep that going on?

514
00:40:28,640 --> 00:40:30,779
Scott: They couldn't because of the kind of story it was.

515
00:40:31,400 --> 00:40:39,240
Scott: And it takes a sharp turn with new characters and a new conflict, and people don't like or care about those new characters and new conflict very much.

516
00:40:39,842 --> 00:40:48,479
Rym: I read like one and a half volumes of the post-swerve Death Note, and I just didn't care and stopped reading it, and I never read the end.

517
00:40:49,260 --> 00:40:51,760
Scott: Well, so you had the most possible extreme reaction.

518
00:40:51,981 --> 00:40:56,839
Rym: I just lost all interest, and later I read a summary of what happened, and I feel like I didn't miss much.

519
00:40:57,760 --> 00:40:58,464
Scott: Not too much, no.

520
00:41:00,072 --> 00:41:01,399
Scott: You could basically stop reading it.

521
00:41:02,081 --> 00:41:03,359
Scott: You could treat that part as the end.

522
00:41:03,721 --> 00:41:04,106
Rym: You know what?

523
00:41:04,126 --> 00:41:05,220
Rym: That would have been a way better ending.

524
00:41:06,823 --> 00:41:24,020
Scott: And so Chainsaw Man, which the initial manga was 11 volumes, and in those 11 volumes it started extremely strong, as strong as you could possibly start, and then it didn't finish as strong, but it wasn't bad.

525
00:41:25,201 --> 00:41:32,704
Scott: It's like at least I wasn't so excited by the end of Chainsaw Man Part 1.

526
00:41:32,704 --> 00:41:34,720
Scott: I thought it was going to be the end of Chainsaw Man.

527
00:41:35,022 --> 00:41:38,520
Scott: They were wrapping everything up, but it's like, nope, that's the end of Part 1.

528
00:41:38,520 --> 00:41:53,800
Scott: I guess, spoiler, but it's not a spoiler, because you can see that volumes 12 and 13 are for sale, and volume 14 will be available in February, so it's not a spoiler, even if you didn't read volumes 1 through 11 yet, which are available in a box set.

529
00:41:54,540 --> 00:41:59,380
Scott: Anyway, I didn't realize that Part 2 was coming so quickly.

530
00:41:59,822 --> 00:42:03,637
Scott: I was like, oh, volume 12 is already out and 13?

531
00:42:03,637 --> 00:42:04,460
Scott: What's going on here?

532
00:42:06,701 --> 00:42:14,323
Scott: And of course, it takes a big swerve, because they wrapped everything up at the end of Part 1.

533
00:42:14,323 --> 00:42:17,460
Scott: I'm like, what could it possibly be in Part 2?

534
00:42:17,460 --> 00:42:19,205
Scott: And what is it in Part 2?

535
00:42:19,205 --> 00:42:23,960
Scott: Well, they introduce a new character who's got more focus than Chainsaw Man at least.

536
00:42:24,140 --> 00:42:26,679
Scott: Not that Chainsaw Man isn't there, he's very, very there.

537
00:42:27,264 --> 00:42:29,500
Scott: His name of the manga is still Chainsaw Man.

538
00:42:31,142 --> 00:42:33,716
Scott: And it's like, will I care?

539
00:42:33,736 --> 00:42:34,480
Scott: And you know what?

540
00:42:35,120 --> 00:42:42,860
Scott: I think volumes 12 and 13 of Chainsaw Man might be better than any of the previous 11 volumes of Chainsaw Man.

541
00:42:43,522 --> 00:42:47,180
Scott: I think they do it better in the second arc than the first arc.

542
00:42:47,300 --> 00:42:51,658
Scott: I care way more about this new character than all those characters in the first 11 volumes.

543
00:42:52,120 --> 00:42:57,140
Scott: I couldn't even remember half their names, and a lot of them got chainsawed anyway, as you would expect.

544
00:42:57,640 --> 00:43:04,040
Rym: I mean, there's a mean time before chainsawing in a comic called Chainsaw Man.

545
00:43:04,840 --> 00:43:04,980
Scott: Right.

546
00:43:05,301 --> 00:43:16,060
Scott: And the character of Chainsaw Man, the character, Denji, is not the reason you like, at least for me, the reason to like the manga.

547
00:43:17,781 --> 00:43:21,500
Scott: It's about, you know, the other characters are much more interesting than him.

548
00:43:21,941 --> 00:43:23,929
Rym: He's doing his best, that character.

549
00:43:23,949 --> 00:43:26,037
Rym: I'll leave it at that.

550
00:43:27,600 --> 00:43:27,760
Scott: Right.

551
00:43:28,061 --> 00:43:34,255
Scott: But the characters that they've created in volume, you know, the main one especially, in volumes 12 and 13, it's like, oh snap.

552
00:43:37,081 --> 00:43:39,207
Scott: I think there's really, this is much better.

553
00:43:39,227 --> 00:43:41,132
Scott: I'm into it.

554
00:43:41,854 --> 00:43:44,140
Scott: I'm waiting for volume 14 in February.

555
00:43:44,300 --> 00:43:54,240
Scott: So, and you know what, here's something else, is that all you really have to know about Chainsaw Man, right, is that they're devils.

556
00:43:54,860 --> 00:43:55,422
Scott: They're bad.

557
00:43:56,948 --> 00:43:59,679
Scott: And like there's a little bit of lore, I guess, that would help you.

558
00:44:00,260 --> 00:44:06,840
Scott: But like, you don't need to know too much about the first 11 volumes to just start reading at volume 12 if you wanted to.

559
00:44:07,983 --> 00:44:14,480
Scott: And it's like, I think you'd be missing out on some knowledge, like how devil pacts work and shit like that.

560
00:44:15,100 --> 00:44:19,268
Scott: Names of people who were in the first 11 volumes and stuff like that.

561
00:44:19,889 --> 00:44:25,320
Scott: But really, other than Chainsaw Man, it's new characters mostly.

562
00:44:28,601 --> 00:44:38,140
Scott: And, you know, you could just do it and pretend, you know, all the mysterious stuff that you don't understand is just like, you know, you're just thrown into the story in the middle.

563
00:44:38,461 --> 00:44:39,700
Scott: You're not meant to understand it yet.

564
00:44:39,720 --> 00:44:40,784
Rym: Fire the low and greens.

565
00:44:40,804 --> 00:44:43,394
Rym: You don't need to know the details of what a low and green is.

566
00:44:43,455 --> 00:44:44,760
Rym: Just, you know, that they can be fired.

567
00:44:45,741 --> 00:44:53,100
Scott: Yeah, so I think it's, you know, I was like, I was just going to check it out because I was curious what could possibly happen in part two.

568
00:44:53,501 --> 00:44:55,511
Scott: I was expecting a Death Note-like swerve.

569
00:44:56,053 --> 00:44:57,380
Scott: It was the exact opposite.

570
00:44:57,540 --> 00:45:08,579
Scott: I've never seen an anime or manga before where the additional arc actually did a hockey stick upwards, right, in terms of, and it was already pretty strong, you know.

571
00:45:10,561 --> 00:45:12,985
Rym: So I got a short one as well.

572
00:45:13,005 --> 00:45:16,311
Rym: I know I've mentioned it a few times because season three dropped, but now we've seen it all.

573
00:45:16,392 --> 00:45:20,139
Rym: Hilda, there are only 34 episodes plus a movie total.

574
00:45:20,159 --> 00:45:20,860
Rym: It's all on Netflix.

575
00:45:21,120 --> 00:45:21,664
Scott: That's a lot.

576
00:45:22,289 --> 00:45:22,894
Scott: Only 34 episodes.

577
00:45:24,000 --> 00:45:28,097
Rym: Well, that's three seasons and each season is a complete story.

578
00:45:28,137 --> 00:45:28,719
Rym: start to finish.

579
00:45:29,581 --> 00:45:33,586
Rym: And it's just like it's an animated TV show based on a comic, a fantasy comic.

580
00:45:34,588 --> 00:45:43,920
Rym: that is the kind of thing that if you didn't know what it was and you saw it at a place like MOCA, you'd go, oh, and just buy it automatically just from the cover and read it.

581
00:45:44,040 --> 00:45:45,126
Rym: And it's really good.

582
00:45:45,146 --> 00:45:47,720
Rym: I have nothing about positive things to say about Hilda.

583
00:45:48,301 --> 00:46:01,800
Rym: But now that it's ended, what I'm surprised by is that it actually does address answers to questions that I never thought would be bothered to be answered.

584
00:46:02,320 --> 00:46:11,340
Rym: Like it raises the stakes and deals with some things that you really wouldn't expect to be dealt with considering the way this comic and the show go up to this point.

585
00:46:12,141 --> 00:46:14,986
Rym: And the end is actually pretty serious.

586
00:46:15,707 --> 00:46:24,260
Rym: The stakes are high enough to where it's pretty dark and fairly tragic, which is a pretty radical departure from how the show goes up to that point.

587
00:46:25,180 --> 00:46:32,220
Rym: And I'd say Hilda, overall, this work edges into the bottom of S-tier.

588
00:46:33,481 --> 00:46:38,969
Rym: This is like A+ tier, maybe the bottom of S-tier.

589
00:46:38,989 --> 00:46:46,059
Rym: I have basically zero complaints about this absolutely delightful show, and I think anyone who has access to Netflix should watch it.

590
00:46:47,520 --> 00:46:47,751
Scott: Interesting.

591
00:46:48,301 --> 00:46:51,319
Rym: I think I'm going to read the comic that it's based on now.

592
00:46:53,823 --> 00:46:55,130
Rym: But yeah, it'll tell a complete story.

593
00:46:55,170 --> 00:46:56,034
Rym: There's no more Hilda.

594
00:46:56,155 --> 00:46:56,999
Rym: It's done, done, done.

595
00:46:58,521 --> 00:47:05,560
Rym: I think if you watch it all, you'll be very satisfied with where it ends and what's going on at the end of the show.

596
00:47:06,320 --> 00:47:15,960
Rym: And the only thing I'll spoil, because the thing that a lot of GeekNights listeners hate the most, and I also hate the most, is when there's a story about magic but also magical realism.

597
00:47:16,381 --> 00:47:20,315
Rym: And at the end, either the magic goes away or it was all a dream.

598
00:47:20,676 --> 00:47:21,880
Scott: None of that shit happens here.

599
00:47:22,360 --> 00:47:31,880
Rym: At the end of the show, everyone involved, including the adults, all agree, yes, all of that happened and the ramifications of it are well known.

600
00:47:32,440 --> 00:47:33,559
Scott: Boy in the Heron did the same thing.

601
00:47:34,381 --> 00:47:38,592
Rym: Yeah, that's a green flag for me in any media.

602
00:47:38,973 --> 00:47:41,700
Rym: Red flag whenever all the magic goes away and it was just a dream.

603
00:47:44,440 --> 00:47:48,484
Scott: Alright, so in terms of comic books lately, it's weird.

604
00:47:49,285 --> 00:48:05,900
Scott: Back in the day, I feel like when I started ordering comic books and going to the comic book store constantly, and when I was living in Beacon, I was sort of catching up on all of the comics I wasn't able to buy and read the earlier part of my life because I didn't A) have money, B) have a comic book store.

605
00:48:07,761 --> 00:48:10,765
Scott: And so I caught up and I filled all these shelves behind me rapidly.

606
00:48:11,306 --> 00:48:18,956
Scott: And then about ordering comics, pre-ordering them every single month for years and years and years, it's been since 2016, so maybe a long time.

607
00:48:22,860 --> 00:48:36,740
Scott: It's sort of like, well, there aren't really that many new series, there's a lot of them, but there aren't so many that are like, really, I'm going to spend money to get a paper version, sight unseen.

608
00:48:40,122 --> 00:48:46,320
Scott: When you look at the things that are worth buying and putting on the shelf, especially when you're low on shelf space, it's like saga level stuff.

609
00:48:48,641 --> 00:48:51,645
Scott: And usually I find out something is at that level way later.

610
00:48:52,246 --> 00:49:01,440
Scott: And so a lot of times I've actually just been ordering nothing some months, and other months I just order a few things, not too many, and then I forget what I ordered.

611
00:49:03,603 --> 00:49:10,391
Scott: And so a box will show up and you'll look inside and it's almost like, "Oh, Pascott, why did you buy

612
00:49:10,411 --> 00:49:10,692
Scott: that?".

613
00:49:10,832 --> 00:49:15,337
Scott: Or, "Oh, Pascott, thank you for buying this.

614
00:49:16,058 --> 00:49:17,260
Scott: I completely forgot about

615
00:49:17,280 --> 00:49:17,320
Scott: it.".

616
00:49:17,320 --> 00:49:23,952
Scott: And so a thing showed up that I was upset that it was so large, and then I opened it up and realized what it was.

617
00:49:24,152 --> 00:49:28,440
Scott: Pascott ordered The Simpsons Treehouse of Horror.

618
00:49:28,743 --> 00:49:29,615
Rym: Oh damn, that's huge!

619
00:49:30,660 --> 00:49:31,802
Scott: It's enormous, right?

620
00:49:32,344 --> 00:49:40,240
Scott: So if you don't know, there are all these Simpsons comics from back in the day that were published by a company called Bongo Comics.

621
00:49:40,901 --> 00:49:43,960
Rym: I have a lot of them, but not all of them.

622
00:49:44,621 --> 00:49:54,320
Scott: They went for a long, long time and made a lot of comics, and as you would expect during the Halloween season, every year they would put out some Treehouse of Horror comics.

623
00:49:56,122 --> 00:50:01,560
Scott: Just wacky Simpsons comics that involved horrors just like the TV show did.

624
00:50:02,920 --> 00:50:04,303
Scott: And so there were so many of those.

625
00:50:05,948 --> 00:50:10,940
Scott: Bongo Comics has been out of business for a while, and there haven't been new Simpsons comics.

626
00:50:11,421 --> 00:50:18,640
Scott: But it looks like Abrams Comic Arts must have the rights or the license or something that allows them to publish this.

627
00:50:19,280 --> 00:50:26,940
Scott: And what they've done is they've taken many Treehouse of Horror comics, and not in some chronological order, by the way.

628
00:50:27,501 --> 00:50:30,180
Scott: It might be like a "best of" or some other arrangement.

629
00:50:30,481 --> 00:50:31,899
Rym: Looks like there's going to be a second one.

630
00:50:32,720 --> 00:50:35,167
Scott: Yeah, it comes in this mega box, right?

631
00:50:35,188 --> 00:50:39,300
Scott: And it's like this goes in, and then you see part of the cover shows in the hole.

632
00:50:40,702 --> 00:50:43,759
Scott: Also, I found out by accident that this cover glows in the dark.

633
00:50:46,081 --> 00:50:55,520
Scott: But the way the book is arranged is that they've got the sci-fi ones, the alien ones, the trick-or-treating ones, the thrillers.

634
00:50:55,720 --> 00:50:59,198
Rym: Oh, I just realized the title of it is "The Ominous

635
00:50:59,218 --> 00:50:59,520
Rym: Omnibus."

636
00:51:01,140 --> 00:51:01,855
Rym: Exactly, yeah.

637
00:51:03,560 --> 00:51:10,260
Scott: And so yeah, this has a lot of Simpsons comics in it, in a very large and fancy book.

638
00:51:10,680 --> 00:51:11,879
Scott: You can see they're all out of order.

639
00:51:12,180 --> 00:51:15,340
Rym: I can say, because I read a bunch of these as they were coming out.

640
00:51:16,081 --> 00:51:22,200
Rym: I remember thinking they were about as funny as The Simpsons, the show, and I remember really liking them.

641
00:51:22,561 --> 00:51:32,264
Rym: There are comics in here as old as 1995 and as new as like 2016?

642
00:51:32,264 --> 00:51:34,079
Rym: '95 I think is when it started?

643
00:51:36,661 --> 00:51:38,639
Scott: Yeah, I think 2016 is the newest year.

644
00:51:38,820 --> 00:51:42,960
Rym: Yup, fall of 1995 is when Bongo started making The Simpsons comics.

645
00:51:44,322 --> 00:51:49,163
Scott: Yup, so yeah, "Call Me Homer" from Treehouse #1 is on page 256.

646
00:51:49,163 --> 00:51:52,400
Scott: And then in the back, there's a creator index.

647
00:51:53,220 --> 00:51:59,240
Scott: So you can look up to see if your favorite artists or writers or creators have made any of these comics.

648
00:51:59,420 --> 00:52:01,159
Scott: So one of them was written by Mark Hamill.

649
00:52:03,221 --> 00:52:05,519
Scott: There's a lot of names on here that you will know.

650
00:52:05,900 --> 00:52:12,700
Rym: I would even say I remember these being as good as how good the My Little Pony comics were when those came out.

651
00:52:13,400 --> 00:52:16,640
Scott: The Simpsons comics are better than the Sergio Aragones one in here.

652
00:52:17,003 --> 00:52:18,839
Scott: You get a wide variety of art styles.

653
00:52:20,322 --> 00:52:25,540
Scott: As long as you know anything about The Simpsons, there's not a continuity of anything.

654
00:52:26,841 --> 00:52:34,800
Rym: Well, specifically Treehouse of Horror episodes are by design and stated as non-canonical compared to The Simpsons.

655
00:52:35,520 --> 00:52:37,360
Scott: Right, but they're all standalone.

656
00:52:37,720 --> 00:52:39,130
Scott: There are a lot of references.

657
00:52:42,042 --> 00:52:42,723
Scott: That's the thing.

658
00:52:42,764 --> 00:52:48,520
Scott: The Simpsons itself, the TV show, is constantly making pop culture references to other things.

659
00:52:49,020 --> 00:52:56,440
Scott: Whereas Simpsons comics obviously do the same thing, but they also make Simpsons references to the TV show.

660
00:52:58,260 --> 00:53:05,499
Scott: Like you'll have a lot of Milhouse parents divorce jokes and things like that going on.

661
00:53:07,361 --> 00:53:13,680
Scott: So if you like Simpsons at all, should you buy a giant hardcover if you have room for it?

662
00:53:14,060 --> 00:53:21,900
Scott: This is actually quite a superior large book to have because it's not one of those coffee table ones you look at once with art.

663
00:53:22,820 --> 00:53:25,379
Scott: And maybe look at a second time if someone visits and notices it.

664
00:53:26,162 --> 00:53:27,439
Scott: You'll actually read this whole thing.

665
00:53:30,805 --> 00:53:32,012
Scott: I don't think it was that expensive.

666
00:53:32,032 --> 00:53:33,440
Scott: What's the actual price on it?

667
00:53:33,540 --> 00:53:34,058
Scott: Forty bucks?

668
00:53:34,701 --> 00:53:35,263
Scott: Forty bucks.

669
00:53:35,303 --> 00:53:36,107
Scott: I paid less than that.

670
00:53:36,247 --> 00:53:39,320
Scott: So I probably had at least fifteen percent or more.

671
00:53:39,400 --> 00:53:43,219
Rym: I paid more for video games that I played for fewer hours than it would take to read this.

672
00:53:44,440 --> 00:53:49,295
Scott: Yeah, so I didn't remember ordering it until it showed up.

673
00:53:49,315 --> 00:53:50,058
Scott: And then I was like,

674
00:53:50,118 --> 00:53:50,379
Scott: "Wow!".

675
00:53:50,942 --> 00:53:52,980
Scott: I didn't realize that this would be that fancy.

676
00:53:53,880 --> 00:53:54,684
Rym: You know what's extra funny?

677
00:53:54,704 --> 00:54:00,999
Rym: Because when you mentioned that you were going to talk about it on this show, what I thought was, "That's not the kind of thing Scott would normally

678
00:54:01,080 --> 00:54:01,140
Rym: buy.".

679
00:54:03,440 --> 00:54:06,798
Rym: I was curious what you were going to say about it as a result.

680
00:54:08,040 --> 00:54:11,460
Scott: I wouldn't buy Bongo comics on the regular when they existed.

681
00:54:11,740 --> 00:54:14,039
Scott: Because it's like, "Do you really need a house full of Simpsons

682
00:54:14,059 --> 00:54:14,180
Scott: comics?".

683
00:54:14,180 --> 00:54:14,802
Rym: I mean, we did.

684
00:54:15,203 --> 00:54:20,640
Rym: But the way we treated floppies was we'd buy them, we'd read them, and we'd either give them away or lose them or throw them away.

685
00:54:21,481 --> 00:54:21,962
Scott: Right.

686
00:54:21,982 --> 00:54:23,225
Scott: But they had trades.

687
00:54:23,546 --> 00:54:29,460
Scott: But the thing is, when it was free comic book day, I would regularly take the Bongo free comic.

688
00:54:30,383 --> 00:54:32,080
Scott: Because you knew that it was going to be quality.

689
00:54:33,381 --> 00:54:36,178
Scott: So this is like, "Hey, here's a compilation for

690
00:54:36,199 --> 00:54:36,299
Scott: you.".

691
00:54:36,420 --> 00:54:37,043
Scott: It's like, "You know what?

692
00:54:37,103 --> 00:54:40,339
Scott: It's been a long time since I read a Bongo comic because you literally went out of

693
00:54:40,379 --> 00:54:40,600
Scott: business.".

694
00:54:42,943 --> 00:54:46,320
Scott: So this is the right dosage of that kind of content.

695
00:54:47,840 --> 00:54:49,885
Rym: Alright, so one last little thing.

696
00:54:49,905 --> 00:54:56,240
Rym: Because part of the reason why I haven't watched as much, like Scott watches less screen than I think I do.

697
00:54:56,781 --> 00:54:59,351
Scott: I've been watching actually a ton of movies lately.

698
00:54:59,371 --> 00:55:01,980
Scott: I just really do not like television.

699
00:55:03,982 --> 00:55:07,854
Rym: We tend to watch specific shows that we have a specific reason to watch.

700
00:55:07,895 --> 00:55:09,540
Rym: that are not long-running shows.

701
00:55:09,700 --> 00:55:11,188
Rym: Like Hilda was a specific show.

702
00:55:11,611 --> 00:55:12,777
Rym: Season comes out, we watch it.

703
00:55:13,741 --> 00:55:21,080
Rym: And Arcane, actually I'm looking forward to the next season of that because that is still one of the best animated TV shows I've ever seen.

704
00:55:23,520 --> 00:55:29,140
Rym: But a lot of what I've been watching lately is short, independent animation.

705
00:55:30,281 --> 00:55:31,567
Rym: Like one-off shorts.

706
00:55:31,748 --> 00:55:34,180
Rym: It's not a series, it's not a movie.

707
00:55:34,440 --> 00:55:39,114
Rym: It's an animation someone made that they uploaded to YouTube and maybe put in a few FIM festivals.

708
00:55:39,174 --> 00:55:41,080
Rym: that's like 4-10 minutes long.

709
00:55:41,260 --> 00:55:41,983
Rym: Like that kind of thing.

710
00:55:42,606 --> 00:55:45,660
Rym: So I want to link to one of those because it was pretty good.

711
00:55:45,760 --> 00:55:55,760
Rym: But full disclosure, if you want to watch this, it deals with way heavier subject matter than the art style might imply.

712
00:55:57,240 --> 00:56:00,720
Rym: So read the description and dig into the details to make sure if you want to watch it.

713
00:56:00,760 --> 00:56:08,480
Rym: Because it's about mental illness, it's about potentially the apocalypse, it's about potentially exploited children.

714
00:56:09,302 --> 00:56:13,200
Rym: And it's just a 9 minute short film called Twins in Paradise.

715
00:56:14,623 --> 00:56:15,491
Scott: It's only 9 minutes?

716
00:56:15,552 --> 00:56:16,319
Scott: I could watch 9 minutes.

717
00:56:17,241 --> 00:56:18,725
Rym: It's not the greatest thing I've seen.

718
00:56:18,805 --> 00:56:23,920
Rym: I picked this one kind of randomly out of a list of like 30 or 40 of them that are in my YouTube likes.

719
00:56:24,580 --> 00:56:32,220
Rym: But this one surprised us and we actually spent some time unpacking what it was about and what was going on with it.

720
00:56:32,960 --> 00:56:35,288
Rym: There's a lot to say about it.

721
00:56:35,569 --> 00:56:38,960
Rym: It's the kind of thing you could assign to a college class and make everyone write an essay on.

722
00:56:41,781 --> 00:56:43,426
Rym: So yeah, I think we've killed enough time.

723
00:56:43,526 --> 00:56:46,935
